# kernel/Kconfig - sourced from a target kernel tree's own Kconfig (e.g.
# drivers/Kconfig: `source "drivers/su_core/Kconfig"` once kernel/ is
# placed there - see ../README.md "Integration"). NOT auto-sourced by
# a vanilla/unmodified kernel tree - this is why this project's CI
# (.github/workflows/kernel-build.yml) passes CONFIG_SU_CORE=m directly
# on the `make` command line instead of relying on this file being
# wired into a vanilla torvalds/linux checkout's own Kconfig tree.

config SU_CORE
	tristate "Su root kernel module"
	depends on ARM64
	default m
	help
	  Kernel-side support for this project's su mechanism, Magisk-
	  compatible module (OverlayFS) loading, and allowlist-based
	  visibility control. See docs/ in the project root for the full
	  design.

	  Say M here (the default) to build as a loadable module - the
	  normal choice for fast iteration during development, and the
	  only option on devices that support loadable kernel modules at
	  all.

	  Say Y to build in (not as a loadable module) - required on
	  devices where loadable modules aren't viable (CONFIG_MODULES
	  disabled in the target kernel, or module-loading signature
	  enforcement that only accepts OEM-signed .ko files). This is
	  what tools/boot_patch/ (see docs/PHASE7_DESIGN.md) exists to get
	  onto a device's boot partition - Y here is a prerequisite for
	  that tool to have anything meaningful to patch in.

	  Say N to exclude entirely.
